Grant BPF syscall privileges for the Android 13 (fydroid) container.

Companion to 097-grant-bpf-privileges-for-android13.patch, which made the
container able to *mount* its own bpffs but not to *use* bpf(). The A13
in-container bpfloader still failed:

  LibBpfLoader: Failed to create maps: (ret=-1) in .../bpf/offload.o
  bpfloader: === CRITICAL FAILURE LOADING BPF PROGRAMS FROM ... ===
  bpfloader: --- DO NOT EXPECT SYSTEM TO BOOT SUCCESSFULLY ---

bpfloader gives up on the whole directory after the first object fails, so it
never reaches netd_shared/, and netd then dies in a restart loop:

  BpfHandler: Failed to get program from
    /sys/fs/bpf/netd_shared/prog_netd_cgroupskb_egress_stats: No such file
  NetdUpdatable: libnetd_updatable_init: BPF handler init failed

which blocks zygote/system_server and leaves the container at bootanim
forever. /system/bin/gpuservice crash-loops on the same cause (libgpuwork
BpfMap over the gpu_mem tracepoint program).

Root cause: the container holds CAP_SYS_ADMIN/CAP_NET_ADMIN only in its own
user namespace, but 6.1 evaluates BPF privileges with capable(), i.e. against
init_user_ns, which a child userns can never satisfy -> EPERM.

Upstream funnels every one of these gates through bpf_token_capable() (v6.9+),
so the fydroid 6.12 patch relaxes exactly one function; 6.1 has no
kernel/bpf/token.c, and its gates route through bpf_capable() instead. This
introduces the equivalent helper with the same semantics as upstream's
bpf_ns_capable() and routes the bpf() gates through it. The bpf_capable()
change covers all ~25 map-allocator call sites in one place, and every
bpf_capable() caller tree-wide is a BPF path (kernel/bpf/, net/core/filter.c,
net/core/bpf_sk_storage.c, include/linux/filter.h).

6.1 additionally keeps the per-map-type CAP_NET_ADMIN checks inside the
individual allocators, which v6.12 lifted into map_create() where the token
gate covers them (6.12 syscall.c gates SOCKMAP, SOCKHASH, DEVMAP,
DEVMAP_HASH and XSKMAP on bpf_token_capable(token, CAP_NET_ADMIN), and its
devmap.c/sock_map.c/xskmap.c carry no capable() call at all). Those four call
sites -- the same five map types -- are routed through the helper too;
Android's tethering offload.o needs it for its DEVMAP_HASH tether_dev_map.

The verifier's five permission flags (bpf_allow_ptr_leaks,
bpf_allow_uninit_stack, bpf_allow_ptr_to_map_access, bpf_bypass_spec_v1,
bpf_bypass_spec_v4 in include/linux/bpf.h) derive from perfmon_capable() on
6.1 and are routed through the helper too; without them the verifier rejects
offload.o with EPERM ("access is allowed only to CAP_PERFMON and
CAP_SYS_ADMIN"). v6.12 gates the same set on bpf_token_capable(token,
CAP_PERFMON), so this matches it.

perfmon_capable() itself is deliberately left alone -- it has non-BPF callers
(perf events, arm_spe_pmu, i915, mali kbase) -- so only the BPF-internal
perfmon gates are relaxed, matching the 6.12 patch's blast radius.
hashtab.c's BPF_F_ZERO_SEED gate keeps its init-ns capable(CAP_SYS_ADMIN),
as v6.12 does.

SECURITY: as with the 6.12 patch, userns root holding CAP_BPF/CAP_SYS_ADMIN
over its own userns can now drive the BPF verifier. This brings the board to
parity with the boards already running fydroid on chromeos-kernel-6_12.

Counterpart of the kernel/bpf/token.c hunk in project-openfyde-patches
chromeos-kernel-6_12 069-grant-bpf-privileges-for-android13.patch.
RE-CHECK ON KERNEL UPREV.

